From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Use a shorter pipeline latency with x264

The pipeline latency is fixed at 500 ms, for OpenH264, whose latency spikes
after scene changes. Every packet is sent that long after it was captured,
so it is felt directly as the receiver lagging behind the desktop.

x264, which is preferred (patch 0001), is configured for zero latency with
no lookahead and no B-frames and has no such spikes. With x264 the latency
is now 150 ms. The other encoders keep 500 ms.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -499,12 +499,23 @@
 {
   GstElement *pipeline;
 
+  WfdMediaFactory *self = WFD_MEDIA_FACTORY (factory);
+  WfdGstElement encoder = wfd_media_factory_profiles[self->factory_profile].video_encoder;
+
   pipeline = GST_RTSP_MEDIA_FACTORY_CLASS (wfd_media_factory_parent_class)->create_pipeline (factory, media);
 
   /* We need a high latency for the openh264 encoder at least when the
    * usage-type is set to "screen". After e.g. scene changes the latency will
-   * be very high for short periods of time, and this prevents further issues. */
-  gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 500 * GST_MSECOND);
+   * be very high for short periods of time, and this prevents further issues.
+   *
+   * Every packet is sent that much after it was captured, so the latency is
+   * felt as the receiver lagging behind the desktop. x264 is configured for
+   * zero latency with no lookahead and no B-frames, and does not have those
+   * spikes, so it gets a much shorter one. */
+  if (encoder == ELEMENT_X264)
+    gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 150 * GST_MSECOND);
+  else
+    gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 500 * GST_MSECOND);
 
   return pipeline;
 }
